ic status as to certain  genes for personal reasons this is the  most recent update which appeared in the  March 2017 issue of Journal of molecular  diagnostics  once we have filtered the germline  variants from the somatic variants we  now have a list that should be  considered cancer specific somatic  variants this list is usually long and  most of the variants are passenger  mutations in other words they're along  for the ride and don't contribute to any  pathology as such these variants are not  durable nor actionable in that they  contribute nothing to the overall  survival of the tumor the TCGA the  Cancer Genome Atlas is project from the  NCI NHGRI that has completed its work  but still releasing dead in the form of  papers and database submissions Civic  which stands for clinical interpretation  of variants and cancer is an expert  crowd-sourced knowledgebase from  Washington University describing  therapeutic prognostic diagnostic and  predisposing relevance of inherited and  somatic variants of all types there are  now databases that coordinate the  variant gene pathway information and now  related to relevant drugs the drug gene  interaction database at Washington  University and the pharmacogenomics  knowledge base farm GK B at Stanford  University are two such databases  currently there are 402 potentially  druggable genes listed in the DTI  database one result that came out of the  TCGA sequencing project was that  different cancers have different  mutation rates this analysis from the  road shows a significant difference  between melanoma and lung cancers shown  on the right hand side compared to  inherited disease shown on the left hand  side of the ground this observation  could have implications quin treating  patience with the new immuno checkpoint  inhibitors with regards to cancer  testing several professional  organizations have established standards  and guidelines to follow in December of  2016 cap amp and s code issue joint  guidelines for t